<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Developer ID build. Adapted from Tovu-Runner's own one-key file, which
  records the measurements that removed the two extra keys electron-builder's default template
  installs. `build/` is electron-builder's `buildResources` directory, whose contents are excluded
  from the packaged app, so this file ships nowhere.

  ONE DIFFERENCE FROM RUNNER MATTERS HERE, and it is the reason this file is not a straight copy.

  Runner's file argues that `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` is unnecessary partly
  because "the .node addons under Contents/Resources/tovu/ are loaded by the child `node` process
  Runner spawns … A spawned binary gets its own code-signing context; this app's entitlements do not
  govern it."

  That argument does NOT transfer. This shell spawns `process.execPath` — the Electron binary
  itself — with `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1` (`src/tovu-server.js`'s `buildCliEnv`), deliberately, so
  that it depends on no system Node install. The child is therefore THIS signed binary running under
  THIS bundle's entitlements and hardened runtime, and it `dlopen`s `better-sqlite3`, `sharp` and
  `argon2` out of `Contents/Resources/tovu/node_modules/`. Whether that succeeds depends on those
  addons carrying a signature from the same Team ID, which is a property of what electron-builder
  signs, not something Runner's reasoning settles.

  MEASURED 2026-09-11 against the signed x86_64 bundle this file produced, and the answer is that
  one key is enough. With this exact file and nothing else, running the packaged app's own binary as
  `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1` from a cwd OUTSIDE the repo:

  * `better-sqlite3` 13.0.3 loaded from `Resources/tovu/node_modules/` and completed a real SQLite
    roundtrip (CREATE/INSERT/SELECT, sqlite 3.53.4).
  * `sharp` 0.35.3 loaded with libvips 8.18.3, and `argon2` loaded.
  * A full `tovu init` + `tovu serve` ran out of the payload: `/`, `/admin/` and
    `/site-chat/site-assistant.js` all answered 200, and the agent daemon came up.

  So `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` is NOT needed and is deliberately absent —
  electron-builder signs the nested `.node` addons under `Resources/` with this same Developer ID,
  which is exactly what library validation requires. It is the single largest weakening available
  here, since it is what otherwise stops arbitrary unsigned code being loaded into the process, so
  do not add it speculatively. If a future dependency IS refused at load, add it together with the
  observed failure that motivated it — the discipline Runner's file applies to the two keys it
  dropped, and this note applies to the key it kept out.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      V8 cannot run without this. The hardened runtime blocks MAP_JIT allocations by default, and
      every Electron process that evaluates JavaScript — main, renderer, and the utility helpers —
      needs them. Without it the process is killed by the kernel on the first JIT allocation,
      before any of this app's own code runs.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
      Under hardened runtime, mic capture is refused without this entitlement — admin's
      `mic-capture.ts` calls `getUserMedia` for voice input in the editor, and without this key
      that call is denied before the NSMicrophoneUsageDescription prompt ever shows.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
